<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaius Maecenas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 70–8 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaius Maecenas was a Roman statesman and friend of Augustus, descended from an Etruscan noble family of Arretium. He acted as Octavian's diplomatic agent and deputy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> during the civil wars, but he is best remembered as patron of poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> and Propertius. His name became a byword for a generous patron of the arts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaius-maecenas.jpg" alt="Gaius Maecenas" width="320" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaius_Cilnius_Maecenas_Roman_Statesman_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
